1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:01,352
Four objects.

2
00:00:01,392 --> 00:00:03,761
So why does
furniture take ‘is’?

3
00:00:04,303 --> 00:00:07,141
A chair, a table,
a sofa and a bed.

4
00:00:07,181 --> 00:00:08,147
I can count them:

5
00:00:08,187 --> 00:00:10,117
one chair,
two chairs.

6
00:00:10,157 --> 00:00:12,138
But furniture
is the overall label

7
00:00:12,178 --> 00:00:13,788
for the whole
collection,

8
00:00:13,828 --> 00:00:16,079
not the name
of each item.

9
00:00:16,603 --> 00:00:18,981
English treats that
word as uncountable.

10
00:00:19,021 --> 00:00:21,053
So: ‘The furniture
is expensive.’

11
00:00:21,093 --> 00:00:22,293
Not ‘are’.

12
00:00:23,237 --> 00:00:24,119
Need a number?

13
00:00:24,159 --> 00:00:25,318
Count pieces:

14
00:00:25,358 --> 00:00:27,988
‘Two pieces of furniture
are expensive.’

15
00:00:28,028 --> 00:00:30,477
The number
goes on pieces,

16
00:00:30,517 --> 00:00:31,957
so the verb
is plural.

17
00:00:32,903 --> 00:00:34,668
Same picture
with luggage:

18
00:00:34,708 --> 00:00:36,032
‘The luggage
is heavy’,

19
00:00:36,072 --> 00:00:38,462
but ‘three pieces
of luggage are heavy’.

20
00:00:38,502 --> 00:00:40,263
See what
the noun names,

21
00:00:40,303 --> 00:00:42,145
then choose
the verb.

